In article <16C29A311.D1SMITH@cmsa.gmr.com> D1SMITH@cmsa.gmr.com writes:
>I have a multivendor cell made up of 5 machines across 3 subnets.
>I have found out the hard way that if your cell spans multiple subnets,
>you MUST specify
>a separate lan-profile in CDS for each one.  However, I have not been able
>to determine how to configure the cell with a lan-profile other than the
>default.  It seems that DTS will not function properly in ALL respects unless
>this configuration is correct.  Any experience with DCE lan-profile and
>cell-profile configuration?  I know that SMIT prompts you for this but,
>the other vendors' dce_config scripts are less flexible and never ask.
>
>Any help or direction would be greatly appreciated.

DTS uses RPC profiles to establish a hierarchy of servers. it divides
the universe of DTS machines into clients (it calls them clerks),
local servers and global servers. Local servers all register the local
TS interface and put there names in a profile entry. Historically this
was /.:/lan-profile (for single cell lans) but it can be anything you
want.
Global servers register the global TS interface and write their names
into /.:/cell-profile. There is only 1 global server set.
How do machines find their local servers? By examining their
individual profiles which are named /.:/hosts/<hostname>/profile.
There will be an entry in the profile with a dummy interface uuid and
a member name that points to the local server set profile. This tells
the dtsd on machine A that its local servers can be found in
/.:/lan22-profile (e.g.).
If clients/servers fail to locate enough local servers, then they will
query /.:/cell-profile for global ones.

