In article <CAzs3I.LFo@fig.citib.com> gjb@fig.citib.com (Greg Brail) writes:
>In article <23912uINNnu9@duca.hi.com> wright@duca.hi.com (David Wright) writes:
>>
>>I'm seeking informed opinion about the usage of DCE threads and other
>>pthreads implementations.  Specifically, I've ported DCE threads to
>>our OSF/1, using kernel threads.  This seems to work fine (after I
>>removed quite a few nasty bugs), but it does have the limitation that
>>we don't support priority setting or policy setting.  
>
>Not all DCE implementations support thread priorities, or even policy
>setting (assuming by that you mean policies for mutex locks and
>condition variables). I regard the thread priority stuff as
>non-portable.

Well, if I read the spec correctly, the priority and policy stuff is
not required of a pthreads implementation.  However, if you decide to
implement one of those features, there's a set of capabilities you
need to include.

Anyway, this answers one of my questions -- for the nonce, not
allowing priority or policy setting doesn't appear to be much of a
limitation.

>>Also, we can't
>>support applications that want to have a helper thread in the
>>background to intercept asynchronously-generated signals (e.g. quit).

>Does this mean you don't support sigwait()? The other DCE
>implementations I've use require the use of sigwait instead of other
>signal-handling interfaces. I usually do this by spawning a thread
>that calls sigwait and waits for a signal (hence the name :-) ). How
>does your threading system support signals? It's awfully useful to be
>able to catch them. I can see restricting usage of signals like SIGVTALRM
>and SIGIO like some DCE implementations do, but reliable DCE programs
>should be able to catch signals like SIGINT and SIGTERM to handle
>reliable shutdown.

We support sigwait(), but only for the original thread in a process.
That's because all asynchronously-generated signals are fed to that
thread.  Any child threads can only receive synchronously-generated
signals (e.g. SIGILL, SIGFPE).  This is how our Mach implementation
(OSF/1) does it.  I have ground my teeth about this more than once,
but for now we're stuck with it.

This is the reason we can't do signal_to_cancel_np; that call wants to
spawn off a child thread to act as a signal interceptor, which in our
setup it is incapable of doing.

So, in conclusion, do you (or anyone else -- don't be shy, speak right
up!) think this is a serious limitation?  I'd hate to go to user
threads now if I don't have to.

