wright@duca.hi.com (David Wright) writes:
>
> [stuff deleted]
>
> We support sigwait(), but only for the original thread in a process.
> That's because all asynchronously-generated signals are fed to that
> thread.  Any child threads can only receive synchronously-generated
> signals (e.g. SIGILL, SIGFPE).  This is how our Mach implementation
> (OSF/1) does it.  I have ground my teeth about this more than once,
> but for now we're stuck with it.
> 
> This is the reason we can't do signal_to_cancel_np; that call wants to
> spawn off a child thread to act as a signal interceptor, which in our
> setup it is incapable of doing.
> 
> So, in conclusion, do you (or anyone else -- don't be shy, speak right
> up!) think this is a serious limitation?  I'd hate to go to user
> threads now if I don't have to.

I think the benefits from using OS threads are substantial, so I would
stay away from user threads if possible.  However, I don't completely
understand why you can't implement a generic sigwait().

Could you implement sigwait() to record the desired signals and wait on
a condition variable?  The main thread then has a handler for all
signals.  When a signal comes into the main thread, you look to see if
any other thread is expecting this signal.  If so, then you wake up that
thread's condition variable.  If not, then you provide the default
signal behavior.

Perhaps I'm missing something, but would this work?  I would expect
programs to call sigwait() on signals like SIGINT (Ctrl-C), and I doubt
users will assume that they must restrict these calls to the initial
thread.  In fact, it's probably more likely that they'll spawn a waiter
thread from which they call sigwait().

Erik Brown
Transarc

