I am well and truly peeved...

Some time ago, we decided to use DCE RPC for the communications portion
of our application.  We initially used a field test version of DCE
from DEC, complete with CDS and security.  We were fairly happy with
how things were going.  Recently, the DEC field test ended, so we have
to buy DCE now.  The prices were quite a shock.  The CDS and security
components each run around $1000, even with heavy discounts.  We're also
moving to HP, and their prices are similar, indicating that these absurd
prices are OSF's fault, rather than anyone else's.  Our application sells
for around $2K/seat (it varies depending on the rest of the configuration).
How the hell am I supposed to persuade my customaers to pay another $2000
for a damn piece of enabling software?  These prices are completely absurd
for workstation seats.  I could maybe see it if some kind of central server
were more expensive, with client licenses being cheaper, but even then it's
out of line.

As soon as we realized how unreasonable the pricing was, we decided to use
string binding, and are now trying to code it...  The problem is, we can't
figure out how to get the mechanism to work.  We're trying to communicate
with VMS.  The endpoint changes every time the server comes up, so we need
to have some way to send the information to the workstations every time the
server starts.  This means either NFS mounting something from every VMS system
to every workstation, doing some kind of automated file transfer, or writing
our own mini nameserver using sockets.  All of these approaches entail nasty
coding and configuration problems which DCE RPC was supposed to save us from.

Am I missing something critical about string bindings, or is the DCE situation
really as screwed up as it seems? I can't believe OSF would be so profoundly
stupid as to price the full package out of the reasonable per-seat cost range,
then not provide a reasonable alternative.  Do you *want* DCE to fail as a
standard for communications software?  If so, you're on the right track.

At this point, I'm looking seriously at ONC RPC, even though the interface
isn't as good, and I detest xdr routines.  At least it works without $2000+
per seat additional cost.  It also doesn't require learning the onerous task
of CDS administration, though I suppose the advantages of CDS outweigh the
administration headaches, if not the cost.

- Brad
