In <CB6uCB.Jzn@sugar.NeoSoft.COM> daniels@NeoSoft.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
>their prices are similar, indicating that these absurd
>prices are OSF's fault

I find this conclusion, based on two price lists, a foolish one to make.
Do you think the fact that Unix does not cost $99/CPU is USL's fault?
You can find out OSF's DCE prices, and then make more informed conclusions,
by contacting <direct@osf.org>.

>The endpoint changes every time the server comes up, so we need
>to have some way to send the information to the workstations every time the
>server starts.

You have a couple of options.  You can use a well-known port number.
This isn't great, of course, but it is what TCP/IP people are
currently used to.  You can use a partial binding, and call rpc_ep_resolve,
letting the rpcd on the host find the dynamic port for you.  This is
better than current alternatives and is similar to Sun's portmapper.
Or, you can use the nameservice and be totally independent of hosts and ports.
	/r$


